{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_<scrubbed>","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011qj3vNcWW6FJrk5VG9h5N2","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Resolve API rate-limit keying","subagent_type":"speckit-pro:domain-researcher","prompt":"You are being dispatched by the speckit-autopilot consensus protocol to research and answer an unresolved Clarify question. Return an evidence-grounded recommendation backed by external documentation and community best practices.\n\n## Question to resolve\n\nQ1: Should the API rate-limit token bucket use per-IP or per-API-key keying?\n\n## Context\n\n- This is a new public endpoint.\n- There is no existing pattern in the codebase; no internal prior art to lean on.\n- The choice is between per-IP keying, per-API-key keying, or a layered combination.\n- This is the **domain** analyst slot in a 2-of-3 consensus round (codebase, spec, domain). Your job is to bring industry best practices and authoritative documentation — not to read this repo's code or spec.\n\n## What to research\n\nCover at minimum:\n1. **Threat model fit** — what each keying strategy protects against (abusive single client vs. distributed/botnet vs. credential sharing) and where each fails.\n2. **Public-endpoint guidance** — how authoritative sources (OWASP API Security Top 10, IETF/RFC drafts on rate limiting like draft-ietf-httpapi-ratelimit-headers, major API providers — Stripe, GitHub, Cloudflare, AWS API Gateway, Google Cloud) recommend keying token buckets for public endpoints.\n3. **Anonymous vs. authenticated traffic** — whether the endpoint being \"public\" implies anonymous (no API key issued) or \"publicly reachable but authenticated.\" This materially changes the answer; flag if the question is ambiguous on this point and give a recommendation conditional on each interpretation.\n4. **Common pitfalls** — NAT/CGNAT collisions for per-IP, key-leak amplification for per-API-key, IPv6 /64 considerations, proxy/CDN client-IP trust (X-Forwarded-For spoofing).\n5. **Layered patterns** — when providers combine both (e.g., per-key as primary, per-IP as a global floor) and the operational tradeoffs.\n\n## Tools\n\nUse Tavily search/extract, Context7 (for any framework-specific docs if relevant — e.g., FastAPI, Express rate-limit middleware, envoy, nginx), WebSearch, and WebFetch as needed. Cite sources by URL.\n\n## Return format\n\nReturn a structured response:\n\n- **Recommendation**: one sentence stating the recommended keying strategy (and any conditional branch).\n- **Confidence**: high / medium / low, with one sentence on why.\n- **Rationale**: 3–6 bullets tying the recommendation to specific cited sources.\n- **Caveats / open assumptions**: anything the consensus synthesizer needs to know (e.g., \"assumes endpoint is anonymous; if authenticated, recommendation flips\").\n- **Sources**: bulleted list of URLs with one-line descriptions.\n\nKeep the response tight — under 500 words of prose plus the source list. The synthesizer will compare your answer against the codebase and spec analysts; clarity and citation density matter more than length."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":"<scrubbed-usage>","diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"<scrubbed-session>","uuid":"<scrubbed-uuid>"}
